38 #edb/ref 17592186344953 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186059813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344970 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186059813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344971 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186059813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344972 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186059813 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344973 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186059814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344964 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186059814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344965 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186059815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344966 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186059815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344968 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186059815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344969 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186059854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345308 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186059854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345309 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186059854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345310 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345352 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345353 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064259 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345136 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064259 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345137 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064259 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345138 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345159 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345185 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345187 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345188 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345190 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345351 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063785 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186064960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344686 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064960 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = x\\ln (x - 4)$. Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})^{′}(0)$." 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186064960 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064960 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=5.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4)+\\frac{x}{x-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4)+\\frac{5}{5-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063791 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186064961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344561 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064961 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^{3} + 2x + 1$. Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})^{′}(1)$." 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186064961 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186064961 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2x+1 & =1 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+2x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345154 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345017 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345018 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068863 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345019 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068864 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345020 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068864 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345022 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068864 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345023 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068864 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345025 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345140 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345141 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345142 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345143 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345134 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186068929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345135 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186069521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186068266 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186069521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344776 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186069521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186069521 1071 "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{1}{x} = \\frac{2 + x}{2x} + \\frac{3}{4x}$?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186069521 1071 "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x}?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186069521 1074 "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $x,2x,$ and $4x.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,2x,4x)=4x.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x} \\\\ 4x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) & =4x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) & =4x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x})+4x\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) & =2\\cdot 2x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x})+4x\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4\\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{x}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{\\cancel{2x}})+\\cancel{4x}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{4x}}) \\\\ 4\\cdot (1) & =2\\cdot (2+x)+3 \\\\ 4 & =2(2+x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2(2+x)+3 \\\\ 4 & =4+2x+3 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-\\frac{3}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345216 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345217 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345189 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345191 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078411 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345192 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345054 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345055 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345092 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078492 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345193 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078493 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345199 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078493 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345201 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078493 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345203 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078501 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345349 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078501 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345350 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078556 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345218 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186078612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345038 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186079212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186078020 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186079212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344843 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186079212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186079212 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=0.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186079212 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 6\\frac{dy}{dx} + 8y = 0$." 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186079212 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+6\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+8{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +4)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =-4.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186083259 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345067 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186083260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345069 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186083285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345116 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087666 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345090 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345220 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345264 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344976 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345268 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345269 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345271 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345272 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344978 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344983 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087732 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345211 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344984 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344985 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186087754 1038 #