se] [17592186088314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344895 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088314 1071 "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $x^{2} + y^{2} = 10$?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088314 1071 "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088314 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10$ represents a circle that is centered at the origin, with radius\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =10 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we disregarded the negative solution for the radius since the radius of a circle must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(0,0)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{10}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086526 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344741 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088317 1071 "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x - 2)^{2} + (y - 3)^{2} = 6$?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088317 1071 "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=6?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088317 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =6 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{6})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=2,$ $b=3,$ and $r=\\sqrt{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(2,3)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{6}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086574 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344947 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088325 1071 "![](images/f1/f17e4718742158f65db70bd25e0ba97fd0fba793b5212eee0ee60072eb4231e9.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y = x$ intersects the circle $(x + 4)^{2} + y^{2} = 8$." 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088325 1071 "![](images/f1/f17e4718742158f65db70bd25e0ba97fd0fba793b5212eee0ee60072eb4231e9.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y=x$ intersects the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=8.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088325 1074 "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=x$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+16+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+2 & =0 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=-2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\ny=x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=x$ intersects the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=8$ at the point $(-2,-2).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086586 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344899 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088327 1071 "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation\n$\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} = 1$?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088327 1071 "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088327 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086598 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344671 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088329 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3.5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{2}^{2}})}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{49}{4})}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086604 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344695 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088330 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086634 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344765 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088335 1074 "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(3,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086694 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344704 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088345 1071 "Express $5 - 10\\sin^{2} 20^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088345 1071 "Express $5-10{sin}^{2}⁡{20}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088345 1074 "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n5(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{20}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={20}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{20}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {20}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n5(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{20}^{∘})=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086741 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344806 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088353 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{9}{4}y=0.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186088353 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 3\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{9}{4}y = 0$." 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186088353 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2})(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2}) & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186092235 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345214 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186092238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345215 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186092316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345065 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186092316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345066 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186096641 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345139 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186096658 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345318 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186096672 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344991 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186096697 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345298 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186096751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344957 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186096751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344958 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186096751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344959 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186096752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344951 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186096752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344952 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186096752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344954 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186096752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344955 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186096752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186344956 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186100826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345255 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186100827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345254 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186100856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345070 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186100856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345071 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186100873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345297 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186100876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345306 13194139