28 17592186045585 true] [17592186152716 1071 "Let $R = [1, 3] \\cdot [1, 3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = x + y + 1$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {1, 2, 3}, P_{2} = {1, 2, 3}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f, P)$." 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152716 1071 "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152716 1074 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/46/46b590842cb6dd1cb994318be3a35ef0f7782542d3a74c4f788180577ab50301.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/19/193d8dc290095a750fe6c7fb6a5d6e75efb342e213f8aabc8fb7921cd335242e.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/a8/a8c9191c43ca304fee01dbb4298c5312f1993ebd25aef266dd9a4828af5f25d5.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=4 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150779 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344742 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152717 1071 "Let $R = [0, 2] \\cdot [0, 2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 5 - x - y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {0, 1, 2}, P_{2} = {0, 1, 2}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f, P)$." 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152717 1071 "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152717 1074 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/3c/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/0f/0fefe892c74e8d391e56ee822f83e7e0fe0110593c9d6a4b93b8cfe1e558ab16.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/fe/fe872f1f1b0307760dcdba6ac6f6be20f197662ac250ab46dc77fa472295906f.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=2 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=3+2+2+1=8.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150791 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344552 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152719 1071 "Let $R = [- 1, 1] \\cdot [- 1, 1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 5 - 3x - y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {-1, 0, 1}, P_{2} = {-1, 0, 1}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f, P)$." 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152719 1071 "Let $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5-3x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={-1,0,1},\\,{P}_{2}={-1,0,1},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the upper Riemann sum $U(f,P).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152719 1074 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/85/859d9d6b15dbd08b4c015816e46986268f92a94770df866a562f3e379bae5365.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{M}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{12}+{M}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-3x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-3x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/4c/4c688c7cf529800b8f1ad075dc70784e40a59163330031b375eaad4b1cdd64c9.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/62/6269ba3c4fd853fff89327ab9e6968e2f52439f5c86c0ec5a21dce1620d9ba5d.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(-1,-1)=9 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(0,-1)=6 \\\\ {M}_{12} & =f(-1,0)=8 \\\\ {M}_{22} & =f(0,0)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =9+6+8+5 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150797 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344932 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152720 1071 "Let $R = [1, 2] \\cdot [2, 3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 4x^{2} + y^{2}$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n$L(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$\nwhere $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$, what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152720 1071 "Let $R=[1,2]\\times [2,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152720 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ increases as $x$ increases on $[1,2]$, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ increases as $y$ increases on $[2,3].$\n\n![](images/67/67daa328cc6ae4cb37be386c327fb4548844fad2adc10476bdf1147c5a5d4218.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/51/51808df96cf7dd295bfa24aa3641a601c821116e1a0014898ffe705036704a53.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i-1},{y}_{j-1})=4{x}_{i-1}^{2}+{y}_{j-1}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{x}_{i-1}^{2}+{y}_{j-1}^{2})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $4{x}_{i-1}^{2}+{y}_{j-1}^{2}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150809 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344884 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152722 1071 "Let $R = [0, 1] \\cdot [0, 1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 5y - x + 3$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n$L(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$\nwhere $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$, what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152722 1071 "Let $R=[0,1]\\times [0,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5y-x+3.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152722 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=5y-x+3$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=5y-x+3$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa57d3e7fed08212f12d4b9d7c2e0a4d33a3aac71c8b8a03d1a9a7364484deb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/c4/c4aa9e96fe72a8fb31ab62ff7eb0695cc64992bb9433be516461915a5c92baff.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j-1})=5{y}_{j-1}-{x}_{i}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(5{y}_{j-1}-{x}_{i}+3)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $5{y}_{j-1}-{x}_{i}+3.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152734 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150881 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152734 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344794 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152734 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152734 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)5ye^{-x}ds$, where $C$ is the section of the curve $y = e^{x}$ for $0 \\le x \\le e$?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152734 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}5y{e}^{-x}\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y={e}^{x}$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le e?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152734 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5y{e}^{-x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{e}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{e}5\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{e}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150953 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344570 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152746 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152746 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(x^{2} + y^{2})ds$, where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = 2\\sin θ$ for $0 \\le θ \\le π$?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152746 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152746 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150959 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152747 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344810 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152747 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = xe^{y}$ along the polar curve $r = 3$ for $0 \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{2}$." 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152747 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the polar curve $r=3$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152747 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(3)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3({e}^{3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152751 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150983 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152751 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344651 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152751 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152751 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = 2y^{2}$ along the curve $y = e^{x}$, $0 \\le x \\le \\ln 3$." 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152751 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$ along the curve $y={e}^{x},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152751 1074 "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({10}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(10\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151139 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344801 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152777 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point $A^{′}$, the image of $A(-1, 1)$ under the action of $T$?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152777 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${A}^{′}$, the image of $A(-1,1)$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152777 1074 "To find the image of $A$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $A:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under