 the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-6 & =\\frac{3-6}{-2-0}(x-0) \\\\ y-6 & =\\frac{3}{2}x \\\\ 2y-12 & =3x \\\\ 3x-2y+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151151 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344601 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152779 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152779 1071 "Consider the line $y = 2x - 5$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152779 1071 "Consider the line $y=2x-5$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152779 1074 "First, notice that the line $y=2x-5$ passes through the points $A(0,-5)$ and $B(1,-3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(5,0)$ and ${B}^{′}(4,2).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{2-0}{4-5}(x-5) \\\\ y & =-2(x-5) \\\\ y & =-2x+10 \\\\ 2x+y-10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151157 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152780 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344922 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152780 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152780 1071 "Consider the line $x = 5$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T$, given by\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152780 1071 "Consider the line $x=5$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152780 1074 "First, notice that the line $x=5$ passes through the points $A(5,0)$ and $B(5,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(-5,0)$ and ${B}^{′}(-4,1).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{1-0}{-4-(-5)}(x-(-5)) \\\\ y & =x+5 \\\\ x-y+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151175 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344537 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152783 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y=0,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186152783 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$y^{″} + 7y^{′} + 12y = 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y = Ae^{-3x} + Be^{-4x}$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$y^{″} + 7y^{′} + 12y = 0, y(0) =-3, y^{′}(0) = 2$." 13194139533428 17592186045585 false] [17592186152783 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-3.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}-4B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3A-4B=2.\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations from above together, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-3 \\\\ -3A-4B=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-10,B=7.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-10{e}^{-3x}+7{e}^{-4x}.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186166906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345239 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186166906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345240 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186166906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345241 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186179673 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345081 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186179673 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345082 13