5493 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344949 1077 "selection" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344949 1079 #edb/ref 17592186342512 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344949 1080 #uuid "ffbc37fc-8913-56b6-aa24-4d3f0156cd73" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344763 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344950 1069 "q-74605" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344950 1071 "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc,$ $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨1,3,6⟩$, $c=⟨1,-2,4⟩$, and  $u=⟨3,19,-10⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344950 1072 #uuid "c8c4efc9-6266-5ebb-8bcb-7388b1494f8e" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344950 1074 "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 19 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s+t=3 \\\\ 3s-2t=19 \\\\ 6s+4t=-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 19 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -28\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -28\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -32\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third row of the augmented matrix now corresponds to the equation $0{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}=-32,$ which has no solution.\n\nSo, there are no values of $s$ and $t$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc=u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $u$ does *not* lie on the plane $Π.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344951 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344863 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344951 1069 "q-74606" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344951 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344951 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z=3 \\\\ -2x+2y-2z=-6 \\\\ 5x-5y+5z=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344951 1072 #uuid "6a82f24c-feb2-5d49-ba95-38a42fc2522b" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344951 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 5 & 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 5 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 5 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344952 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344877 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344952 1069 "q-74608" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344952 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344952 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y-6z=4 \\\\ 6x+12y-18z=12 \\\\ -10x-20y+30z=-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344952 1072 #uuid "192e5819-2578-5d5d-bbaf-226710263b1b" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344952 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -10 & -20 & 30 & -20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -10 & -20 & 30 & -20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -10 & -20 & 30 & -20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & -20 & 30 & -20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344953 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344514 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344953 1069 "q-74611" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344953 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344953 1071 "if ${z}_{1}=3-5\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=1+2\\text{i}$, then ${z}_{1}$ divided by ${z}_{2}$ equals" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344953 1072 #uuid "a108f103-e65d-550f-b9ce-798a432fce27" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344953 1074 "First, we set up the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3-5\\text{i}}{1+2\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is $1+2\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $1-2\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $1-2\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}_{1}}{{z}_{2}} & =\\frac{3-5\\text{i}}{1+2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-5\\text{i}}{1+2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1-2\\text{i}}{1-2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6\\text{i}-5\\text{i}+10{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-{2}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-11\\text{i}+10(-1)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-11\\text{i}-10}{1+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7-11\\text{i}}{5}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-7-11\\text{i}}{5}=-\\frac{7}{5}-\\frac{11}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344713 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344954 1069 "q-74612" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344954 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-10y-5z=10 \\\\ -10x+100y+50z=-100 \\\\ -4x+40y+20z=-40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344954 1072 #uuid "f9c421da-e7e3-5854-9928-6f8234b711f0" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344954 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ -10 & 100 & 50 & -100 \\\\ -4 & 40 & 20 & -40\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ -10 & 100 & 50 & -100 \\\\ -4 & 40 & 20 & -40\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 40 & 20 & -40\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & -5 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344908 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344955 1069 "q-74613" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344955 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x-6y+15z=-3 \\\\ 6x+12y-30z=6 \\\\ -6x-12y+30z=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344955 1072 #uuid "78ef528f-61bb-5594-b0fa-7d95e275c2ae" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344955 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 & 15 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -30 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 & 15 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -30 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 12 & -30 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 30 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-5z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-2y+5z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-2y+5z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344641 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344956 1069 "q-74615" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344956 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+4z=1 \\\\ -x-2y-4z=-1 \\\\ 3x+6y+12z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344956 1072 #uuid "92c4fdf6-1e00-5c31-887f-23716f47f72f" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344956 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 12 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+4z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-2y-4z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-2y-4z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344893 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344957 1069 "q-74617" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344957 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z=3 \\\\ x+2y-2z=0 \\\\ -2x+2y-2z=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344957 1072 #uuid "6b30e629-112f-508e-9e77-096b0e33781a" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344957 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344821 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344958 1069 "q-74627" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344958 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-5y+5z=-1 \\\\ -2x+10y=-4 \\\\ x-5y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344958 1072 #uuid "088ddf76-b8ab-5fdd-9fea-d3099bd8dbb5" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344958 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+5z=-3 \\\\ y+z=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-3-5z \\\\ y=-z-1 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3-5z \\\\ -z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344623 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344959 1069 "q-74629" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344959 1071 "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4x-12y+12z=-4 \\\\ 5x+16y-9z=4 \\\\ 2x+6y-6z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344959 1072 #uuid "920a4776-0058-5c9a-a43b-cd0f1925df4a" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344959 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -12 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 16 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -12 & 12 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 16 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 16 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -21 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-21z=4 \\\\ y+6z=-1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=21z+4 \\\\ y=-6z-1 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}21z+4 \\\\ -6z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344719 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344960 1069 "q-7463" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344960 1071 "![](images/b5/b5a933346a903b6712b3df53fa2f241b070ef80146078b96b8f4360ae6650233.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344960 1072 #uuid "c7d8403d-1335-583d-b92f-e71dbc01f5b3" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344960 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $(0,0)$ and has radius $r=3.$ So, the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344557 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344961 1069 "q-74633" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344961 1071 "Consider the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{2}^{n+4}}{{x}^{n}},$ where $x$ is a positive real number and $n\\ge 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344961 1072 #uuid "9da9be74-20cf-54df-89a0-bc02d03f9903" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344961 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form ${a}_{n}=a\\cdot {r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{2}^{n+4}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{n}\\cdot {2}^{4}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{n}}{{x}^{n}} \\\\  & =16{(\\frac{2}{x})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0<r\\le 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0<\\frac{2}{x}\\le 1\\,⟹\\,x\\ge 2.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344630 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344962 1069 "q-74635" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344962 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & -2x+4y+3z=0 \\\\  & x+3y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system has no solution\n- The system is consistent independent\n- The system is consistent dependent" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344962 1072 #uuid "edc80b1b-d469-5071-b24d-3c90bb47fce6" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344962 1074 "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means that  we can find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344811 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344963 1069 "q-74637" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344963 1071 "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+3y=-1 \\\\  & x-2y=1 \\\\  & -x-5y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system has no solution\n- The system is consistent independent\n- The system is consistent dependent" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344963 1072 #uuid "b0089d19-a4b5-5ec4-beaa-917a294458ba" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344963 1074 "To classify the system, we first try to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=1 \\\\ 7y=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=-\\frac{3}{7}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-2(-\\frac{3}{7})=1\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\n \nSo, we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344586 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344964 1069 "q-74640" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344964 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344964 1071 "$\\frac{5\\text{i}}{2+\\text{i}}=$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344964 1072 #uuid "9447c468-5c36-5ebd-a7ab-5b8eb4527c95" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344964 1074 "The denominator is $2+\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $2-\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $2-\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\text{i}}{2+\\text{i}} & =\\frac{5\\text{i}}{2+\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{2-\\text{i}}{2-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\text{i}-5{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{2}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\text{i}-5(-1)}{4-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\text{i}+5}{4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+10\\text{i}}{5}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5+10\\text{i}}{5}=\\frac{5}{5}+\\frac{10}{5}\\text{i}=1+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344812 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344965 1069 "q-74644" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344965 1071 "$\\frac{15\\text{i}}{1+2\\text{i}}=$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344965 1072 #uuid "025b8625-904e-53ba-97d9-5266cb37165b" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344965 1074 "The denominator is $1+2\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $1-2\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $1-2\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15\\text{i}}{1+2\\text{i}} & =\\frac{15\\text{i}}{1+2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1-2\\text{i}}{1-2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\text{i}-30{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-{2}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\text{i}-30(-1)}{1-4(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\text{i}+30}{1+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{30+15\\text{i}}{5}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{30+15\\text{i}}{5}=\\frac{30}{5}+\\frac{15}{5}\\text{i}=6+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344523 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344966 1069 "q-74645" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344966 1071 "$\\frac{2}{5-3\\text{i}}=$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344966 1072 #uuid "01225d36-30f7-596f-9b3b-f0c1052e4c84" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344966 1074 "The denominator is $5-3\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $5+3\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $5+3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5-3\\text{i}} & =\\frac{2}{5-3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{5+3\\text{i}}{5+3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+6\\text{i}}{25+15\\text{i}-15\\text{i}-9{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+6\\text{i}}{25-9{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+6\\text{i}}{25-(9)(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+6\\text{i}}{25+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+6\\text{i}}{34}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10+6\\text{i}}{34}=\\frac{10}{34}+\\frac{6}{34}\\text{i}=\\frac{5}{17}+\\frac{3}{17}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344670 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344967 1069 "q-74646" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344967 1071 "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=2 \\\\  & 2x+(k+1)y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344967 1072 #uuid "a0c28bd7-70de-59aa-93c9-933cbaca4593" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344967 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-7 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+4y=2 \\\\ (k-7)y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=7,$ then the second equation is $0=-1,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=7.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344936 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344968 1069 "q-74648" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344968 1071 "$\\frac{9}{-3+2\\text{i}}=$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344968 1072 #uuid "35212040-01f3-5be8-a8f4-ec8be3d798dd" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344968 1074 "The denominator is $-3+2\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $-3-2\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-3-2\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{-3+2\\text{i}} & =\\frac{9}{-3+2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-3-2\\text{i}}{-3-2\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-27-18\\text{i}}{9+6\\text{i}-6\\text{i}-4{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-27-18\\text{i}}{9-4(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-27-18\\text{i}}{9+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-27-18\\text{i}}{13}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-27-18\\text{i}}{13}=-\\frac{27}{13}-\\frac{18}{13}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344814 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344969 1069 "q-74649" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344969 1071 "$\\frac{-10}{4+3\\text{i}}=$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344969 1072 #uuid "52b139e8-6f1c-52bf-87ab-dbe490ac53e8" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344969 1074 "The denominator is $4+3\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $4-3\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $4-3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-10}{4+3\\text{i}} & =\\frac{-10}{4+3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{4-3\\text{i}}{4-3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-40+30\\text{i}}{16-12\\text{i}+12\\text{i}-9{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-40+30\\text{i}}{16-9{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-40+30\\text{i}}{16-(9)(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-40+30\\text{i}}{16+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-40+30\\text{i}}{25}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-40+30\\text{i}}{25}=-\\frac{40}{25}+\\frac{30}{25}\\text{i}=-\\frac{8}{5}+\\frac{6}{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344648 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344970 1069 "q-74653" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344970 1071 "If ${z}_{1}=5-2\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=3\\text{i}$, then ${z}_{1}$ divided by ${z}_{2}$ equals" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344970 1072 #uuid "205e8dc8-a862-5058-a749-305018b1708a" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344970 1074 "First, we set up the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5-2\\text{i}}{3\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is the imaginary number $3\\text{i}.$ Note that this can be written as $0+3\\text{i},$ so the conjugate is $0-3\\text{i},$ or simply $-3\\text{i}.$\n\nSo, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}_{1}}{{z}_{2}} & =\\frac{5-2\\text{i}}{3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-2\\text{i}}{3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-3\\text{i}}{-3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-15\\text{i}+6{\\text{i}}^{2}}{-9{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-15\\text{i}+6(-1)}{-9(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-15\\text{i}}{9}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-6-15\\text{i}}{9}=-\\frac{6}{9}-\\frac{15}{9}\\text{i}=-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{5}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344560 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344971 1069 "q-74654" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344971 1071 "$\\frac{6+\\text{i}}{4\\text{i}}=$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344971 1072 #uuid "b2a37faa-773a-5a2a-93ff-f02dee230310" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344971 1074 "Here, the denominator is the imaginary number $4\\text{i}.$ Note that this can be written as $0+4\\text{i},$ so the conjugate is $0-4\\text{i},$ or simply $-4\\text{i}.$\n\nSo, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-4\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+\\text{i}}{4\\text{i}} & =\\frac{6+\\text{i}}{4\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-4\\text{i}}{-4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24\\text{i}-4{\\text{i}}^{2}}{-16{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24\\text{i}-4(-1)}{-16(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-24\\text{i}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4-24\\text{i}}{16} & =\\frac{4}{16}-\\frac{24}{16}\\text{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344897 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344972 1069 "q-74658" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344972 1071 "If ${z}_{1}=-2-7\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=6\\text{i}$, then ${z}_{1}$ divided by ${z}_{2}$ equals" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344972 1072 #uuid "8a81102a-0be1-530b-ba51-5c9802f6a8e5" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344972 1074 "First, we set up the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{6\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is the imaginary number $6\\text{i}.$ Note that this can be written as $0+6\\text{i},$ so the conjugate is $0-6\\text{i},$ or simply $-6\\text{i}.$\n\nSo, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-6\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}_{1}}{{z}_{2}} & =\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{6\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-7\\text{i}}{6\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-6\\text{i}}{-6\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\text{i}+42{\\text{i}}^{2}}{-36{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\text{i}+42(-1)}{-36(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-42+12\\text{i}}{36}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-42+12\\text{i}}{36}=-\\frac{42}{36}+\\frac{12}{36}\\text{i}=-\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{1}{3}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344664 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344973 1069 "q-74659" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344973 1071 "If ${z}_{1}=1-12\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=8\\text{i}$, then ${z}_{1}$ divided by ${z}_{2}$ equals" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344973 1072 #uuid "ab6dbce6-4cfe-5744-98ef-0222843b488e" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344973 1074 "First, we set up the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-12\\text{i}}{8\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is the imaginary number $8\\text{i}.$ Note that this can be written as $0+8\\text{i},$ so the conjugate is $0-8\\text{i},$ or simply $-8\\text{i}.$\n\nSo, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-8\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}_{1}}{{z}_{2}} & =\\frac{1-12\\text{i}}{8\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-12\\text{i}}{8\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-8\\text{i}}{-8\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\text{i}+96{\\text{i}}^{2}}{-64{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\text{i}+96(-1)}{-64(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-96-8\\text{i}}{64}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-96-8\\text{i}}{64}=-\\frac{96}{64}-\\frac{8}{64}\\text{i}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344736 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344974 1069 "q-74671" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344974 1071 "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*If $x$ is a vector such that $-x+w=w$ for some $w\\in V$, then $x=0$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ x+(-x+w) & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{adding }x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (x+(-x))+w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0+w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(-w) & =x+w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{adding }-w\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(-w) & =x+(w+(-w)) &  & [\\,\\text{axioms  A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =x+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344974 1072 #uuid "c4dc6297-c39f-58c6-a8af-023dc6a275e2" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344974 1074 "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+w & =w &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ x+(-x+w) & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{adding }x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (x+(-x))+w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A2}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0+w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ w & =x+w &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A3}\\,] \\\\ w+(-w) & =x+w+(-w) &  & [\\,\\text{adding }-w\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ w+(-w) & =x+(w+(-w)) &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A2}\\,] \\\\ 0 & =x+0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A4}\\,\\,] \\\\ 0 & =x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom  A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344716 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344975 1069 "q-74688" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344975 1071 "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+2y=-4 \\\\  & 3x+2ky=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344975 1072 #uuid "bdfa86e8-f2dc-5d19-9144-98ae43ab2ede" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344975 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2k & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2k & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2k-6 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-4 \\\\ (2k-6)y=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=3,$ then the second equation is $0=10,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=3.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344809 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344976 1069 "q-7469" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344976 1071 "Express $\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{5}{sin}^{2}⁡{42}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344976 1072 #uuid "9aec5fcf-a57f-59cb-ba8e-4d66452e89ff" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344976 1074 "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{42}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={42}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{42}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {42}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{84}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{42}^{∘}) & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{84}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344626 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344977 1069 "q-74695" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344977 1071 "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 4x+2y=-8 \\\\  & x+ky=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344977 1072 #uuid "db41cb50-724f-5af7-85df-697ff3307e10" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344977 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & k & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & k & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & -2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2-4k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=2 \\\\  & (2-4k)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{1}{2},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=2.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344798 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344978 1069 "q-7470" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344978 1071 "Find the exact value of ${cos}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘}).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344978 1072 #uuid "e8e88b3f-3c2d-5ee4-b86c-d4867f649b19" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344978 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={75}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {75}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344672 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344979 1069 "q-74715" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344979 1071 "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-3-2\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344979 1072 #uuid "353be5b6-f62d-5474-a5e8-6fa7ab3f0134" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344979 1073 true 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344979 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/0c/0c6548a95b2a81c7dcde33ead78ee0be8d5cee936edc1e2a98c2d59d259404b7.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})≈0.588\\,00.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.588\\,00≈3.73\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344933 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344980 1069 "q-74716" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344980 1071 "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-3-4\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344980 1072 #uuid "50de5bf4-f172-5683-bb63-c927e8858eff" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344980 1074 "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/7e/7eff12ed4bca73dc27f834b0740b5b2235440250e11427f911c8fc29c99fa1d7.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <arg⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =arctan⁡(\\frac{4}{3})≈0.927\\,30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.927\\,30≈4.07\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344755 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344981 1069 "q-74720" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344981 1071 "The point $U$ has Cartesian coordinates $(1,-\\sqrt{3},2).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates of $U?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344981 1072 #uuid "cba0666a-04f8-5761-91d5-608acf1e745f" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344981 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(1,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi $ to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $U$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344815 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344982 1069 "q-74725" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344982 1071 "The point $S$ has Cartesian coordinates $(7,\\sqrt{2},7).$ What are the spherical polar coordinates $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ of $S?$ Express $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ in radians rounded to three decimal places." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344982 1072 #uuid "da345849-4daa-55eb-8df7-62d01de63486" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344982 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+2+49} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(x,y)=(7,\\sqrt{2})$ lies in the first quadrant,  we can find $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{7}) \\\\  & ≈0.199\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{10}) \\\\  & ≈0.795.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $S$ are $(10,0.199,0.795).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344789 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344983 1069 "q-7473" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344983 1071 "Find the exact value of $1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344983 1072 #uuid "b81c92b6-aaf0-5164-b094-1fd4bab4c1a6" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344983 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={22.5}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {22.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344705 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344984 1069 "q-74769" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344984 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(-2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T({v}_{1}).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344984 1072 #uuid "2c55eb86-992c-5929-994c-e27a136f03fe" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344984 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =-2T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] & =-2T({v}_{1})+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -2T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -2T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344518 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344985 1069 "q-74771" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344985 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T({v}_{1}+5{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$, then find $\\,T({v}_{2}).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344985 1072 #uuid "74809f1e-86dc-5e20-b04f-3f1424b7f6d1" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344985 1074 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v+kw)=T(v)+T(kw)=T(v)+kT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}+5{v}_{2}) & =T({v}_{1})+5T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+5T({v}_{2}) \\\\ 5T({v}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 5T({v}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344881 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344986 1069 "q-74773" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186344986 1071 "Consid