ac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{e}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344762 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345066 1069 "q-75276" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345066 1071 "![](images/75/7580d53db6063a859e46addc9635b958e96ad798508dccb0705701d5b36c12b6.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )$ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )).\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345066 1072 #uuid "7512c521-fa54-50d2-9c6a-f910f597d063" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345066 1074 "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344748 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345067 10