we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ))}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344807 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345069 1069 "q-75302" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345069 1071 "A particle $P$ is moving in the $x$-$y$ plane. Its position vector relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345069 1072 #uuid "af6a3602-6598-5ad6-8883-59a5609544bc" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345069 1074 "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ),4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4(0),4(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=\\frac{