 respect to $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\boxed{}\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$\n\nwhere $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ what is the missing expression in the double summation above?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345088 1072 #uuid "ec7e99a6-777f-571d-b63c-0d29f484d92e" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345088 1074 "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion ${R}_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$ over the $x$-axis and ${y}_{i-1}$ and ${y}_{i}$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below. \n\n![](images/88/88ffe30ae5401e00502b35d851b216768aad69bf94236561617adf23e1ce058f.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x,y)=7y-13x+87$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=7y-13x+87$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa57d3e7fed08212f12d4b9d7c2e0a4d33a3aac71c8b8a03d1a9a7364484deb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/c4/c4aa9e96fe72a8fb31ab62ff7eb0695cc64992bb9433be516461915a5c92baff.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{ij} & =f({x}_{i},{y}_{j-1})=7{y}_{j-1}-13{x}_{i}+87\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $1\\le i\\le m$ and $1\\le j\\le n.$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(7{y}_{j-1}-13{x}_{i}+87)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $7{y}_{j-1}-13{x}_{i}+87.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344726 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345089 1069 "q-75407" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345089 1071 "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=1$, if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(1)=-i+j+\\frac{1}{2}k,\\,{q}^{′}(1)=j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345089 1072 #uuid "e872eb2a-80c0-5fe7-b915-1fedf1110b20" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345089 1074 "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4t}{({t}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{4(1)}{({1}^{2}+2)^{2}}\\cdot q(1)+\\frac{2}{{1}^{2}+2}\\cdot {q}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}(-i+j+\\frac{1}{2}k)+\\frac{2}{3}(j+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\,j+\\frac{4}{9}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{2}+{(\\frac{2}{9})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{81}+\\frac{4}{81}+\\frac{16}{81}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{81}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(1) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{4}{9}\\,i+\\frac{2}{9}\\,j+\\frac{4}{9}\\,k}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{2}{3}q(1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-i+j+\\frac{1}{2}k)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i\\cdot \\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j\\cdot j+\\frac{1}{3}\\,k\\cdot k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344866 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345090 1069 "q-7541" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345090 1071 "Find the $y$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=64.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345090 1072 #uuid "e288957c-e18b-52b2-a2c2-dff841d41c69" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345090 1074 "We must have $x=0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =64 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =64 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =64 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{64} \\\\ y & =\\pm 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,-8)$ and $(0,8),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/90/90fc285151a5f549992e3c40ba6cff89532398808d6194fdf7922108b73646ef.png)" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344622 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345091 1069 "q-75448" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345091 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345091 1071 "The inverse of the matrix $Y$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to the blank spaces?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345091 1072 #uuid "6a57d2de-dfa3-5922-be9d-d7417adc9dfc" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345091 1074 "We write down $Y$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $Y$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Y\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${Y}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Y}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344661 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345092 1069 "q-75458" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345092 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345092 1071 "Find ${F}^{′}(e)$ if $F(x)={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345092 1072 #uuid "c985bafb-2593-5b73-b834-81a6aec3295e" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345092 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{a}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{u}{2+{u}^{2}})+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}})+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2x+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${F}^{′}(e).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}{2e+e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2e+e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344935 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345093 1069 "q-75472" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345093 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345093 1071 "Calculate the product ${I}_{2}G$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$G=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345093 1072 #uuid "062bb064-038f-55e1-92f0-0d30d2ec231e" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345093 1074 "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $G$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\n{I}_{2}\\cdot G=G=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344534 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345094 1069 "q-75473" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345094 1071 "Calculate the product $J{I}_{2}$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$J=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345094 1072 #uuid "ae364f6e-5a7d-5d28-b89b-7967cff2127c" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345094 1074 "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $J$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\nJ\\cdot {I}_{2}=J=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344533 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345095 1069 "q-75479" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345095 1071 "Calculate the product $Q{I}_{2}$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345095 1072 #uuid "e7cd9fb4-2876-5e19-b299-1938e7390f38" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345095 1074 "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $Q$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\nQ\\cdot {I}_{2}=Q=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344740 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345096 1069 "q-75490" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345096 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2y+5x=3z+2 \\\\ 3x-6y=5 \\\\ 2x-3y+5z=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & a & -3 \\\\ 3 & -6 & b \\\\ 2 & c & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345096 1072 #uuid "92f08978-289e-50c6-86d9-b99da259f151" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345096 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y-3z=2 \\\\ 3x-6y+0z=5 \\\\ 2x-3y+5z=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,\\,b=0,\\,c=-3.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344708 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345097 1069 "q-75491" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345097 1071 "The unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t).\n$$\n\nFind the principal normal vector at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨3,0,-4⟩,\\,{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345097 1072 #uuid "b15f4c73-c376-5fbd-b671-915cb2fd0f2a" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345097 1074 "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{5}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{5}\\cdot {q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{q}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖ & =\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{‖{T}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{5}⟨0,1,0⟩}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{5}q(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,0,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344926 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345098 1069 "q-75496" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345098 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345098 1072 #uuid "21485ec9-747a-5129-ba2c-794c570827bf" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345098 1074 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344610 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345099 1069 "q-75500" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345099 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2y+3z=7 \\\\ x+z=-1 \\\\ 3z-2x+y=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & b & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345099 1072 #uuid "ef81a3db-1366-5103-83a1-fc5b71b87fc0" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345099 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}0x-2y+3z=7 \\\\ x+0y+z=-1 \\\\ -2x+y+3z=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=0,\\,b=0,\\,c=3.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344535 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345100 1069 "q-75501" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345100 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345100 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5y-3z+x=2 \\\\ 3z+7x-2y=5 \\\\ 2x-2y=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & b & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345100 1072 #uuid "341d799b-3253-576e-be86-8fe6007e3696" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345100 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+5y-3z=2 \\\\ 7x-2y+3z=5 \\\\ 2x-2y+0z=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,\\,b=-2,\\,c=0.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344655 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345101 1069 "q-75503" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345101 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345101 1072 #uuid "9438fd33-674b-505b-988d-ed78456eace8" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345101 1074 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344511 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345102 1069 "q-75506" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345102 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-y+z=1 \\\\ 2z+y+x=-1 \\\\ 3y-z+x=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & b \\\\ 1 & c & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345102 1072 #uuid "c8162803-a4d6-5f94-9721-2c47db8e7ec4" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345102 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}0x-y+z=1 \\\\ x+y+2z=-1 \\\\ x+3y-z=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=0,\\,b=2,\\,c=3.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344538 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345103 1069 "q-75508" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345103 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345103 1072 #uuid "d8b28abc-ffbd-5f2b-b51a-6be08eb05498" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345103 1074 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only\"." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344532 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345104 1069 "q-75509" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345104 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2y-5x+3z=-1 \\\\ x-3y=3 \\\\ -3y+2z-x=-5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & a & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & b \\\\ c & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345104 1072 #uuid "48c6e4a4-32de-5c7d-8a20-ab1e8b1328a5" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345104 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5x+2y+3z=-1 \\\\ x-3y+0z=3 \\\\ -x-3y+2z=-5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,\\,b=0,\\,c=-1.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344795 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345105 1069 "q-75511" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345105 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y+z=-1 \\\\ z+3x-2y=3 \\\\ -y+2z-x=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & b & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345105 1072 #uuid "44c191cc-5551-5e7a-ae21-977f62761f09" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345105 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y+z=-1 \\\\ 3x-2y+z=3 \\\\ -x-y+2z=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-1,\\,b=-2,\\,c=2.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344507 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345106 1069 "q-75512" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345106 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2z+x-5y=1 \\\\ -y+5z-4x=-2 \\\\ 2x-6y-z=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & a \\\\ b & -1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & c & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345106 1072 #uuid "a3ed22e6-6cd3-5798-bf97-54dcbca1e0d4" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345106 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x-5y-2z=1 \\\\ -4x-y+5z=-2 \\\\ 2x-6y-z=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -6 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=-2,\\,b=-4,\\,c=-6.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344869 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345107 1069 "q-75513" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345107 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}z-5x+2y=0 \\\\ z=1-2x \\\\ 7y-3x-2z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & a & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 7 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345107 1072 #uuid "0508a871-572c-5538-b5f6-f882422cda62" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345107 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5x+2y+z=0 \\\\ 2x+0y+z=1 \\\\ -3x+7y-2z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2,\\,b=1,\\,c=-2.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344840 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345108 1069 "q-75521" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345108 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345108 1072 #uuid "2a9495b4-c709-53eb-875a-846142be09cd" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345108 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t}\\,j]\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{4} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=\\pm 1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1)=1\\,i+\\frac{1}{1}\\,j=\\,i+j, \\\\ r(-1)=(-1)\\,i+\\frac{1}{(-1)}\\,j=-\\,i-j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344826 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345109 1069 "q-75524" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345109 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,t\\in [0,\\pi )$ find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345109 1072 #uuid "4ed3b4a4-4334-5910-b956-ca1595cafddd" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345109 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0 \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in [0,\\pi )$, the only solutions are $t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0⟩=⟨0\\,,\\,3⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨1\\,,\\,0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the points $(0,3)$ and $(1,0).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344530 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345110 1069 "q-75586" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345110 1071 "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345110 1072 #uuid "2d3a87ac-015d-5ac1-b497-a6ce1ac3caad" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345110 1074 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344874 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345111 1069 "q-75589" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345111 1071 "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345111 1072 #uuid "ad6b2adc-bc53-5c79-b5cd-55ba86c3dfdd" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345111 1074 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}11=3\\lambda  \\\\ 13=-2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344602 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345112 1069 "q-75595" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345112 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},\\,$ ${R}_{1}:=4{R}_{1},\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345112 1072 #uuid "fd25b025-461f-556b-97f3-6071c19d59d7" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345112 1074 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344859 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345113 1069 "q-75599" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345113 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},\\,$ ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345113 1072 #uuid "1a52696c-f90c-56a7-94f5-6062f59efbae" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345113 1074 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only\"." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344609 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345114 1069 "q-75610" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345114 1071 "An eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]$ \ncorresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =5k-4$ is $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345114 1072 #uuid "e32f6312-bf7a-5fb4-bf70-bbf6dad31c7b" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345114 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}] & =(5k-4)[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2+2(3k-1) \\\\ -10+8(3k-1)\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2(5k-4) \\\\ (5k-4)(3k-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6k \\\\ 24k-18\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10k-8 \\\\ 15{k}^{2}-17k+4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6k=10k-8 \\\\ 24k-18=15{k}^{2}-17k+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n6k=10k-8\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24k-18 & =15{k}^{2}-17k+4 \\\\ 24\\cdot 2-18 & =15\\cdot {2}^{2}-17\\cdot 2+4 \\\\ 30 & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=2.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344888 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345115 1069 "q-75611" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345115 1071 "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 20-4k & 3\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2k+1$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345115 1072 #uuid "2ba66124-4bc1-5252-bf4a-2277af1637ea" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345115 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 20-4k & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =(2k+1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 26-4k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2k+1 \\\\ 4k+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7=2k+1, \\\\ 26-4k=4k+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n7=2k+1\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=3$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}26-4k & =4k+2 \\\\ 26-4\\cdot 3 & =4\\cdot 3+2 \\\\ 14 & =14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=3.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344917 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345116 1069 "q-75627" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345116 1071 "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $6.4+4.8+3.6+2.7+⋯$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345116 1072 #uuid "a8f4028a-246c-50e3-865c-b5e1b5036ff0" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345116 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=6.4$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4.8}{6.4}=0.75.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.4}{1-0.75} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.4}{0.25} \\\\  & =25.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344675 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345117 1069 "q-75635" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345117 1071 "Calculate $CB+CA$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345117 1072 #uuid "80c9e376-8c41-538a-b47f-6281460b7dd2" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345117 1074 "We apply the distributive law \n\n$$\nCB+CA=C(B+A).\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $B+A$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B+A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CB+CA & =C(B+A) \\\\  & =C\\cdot 4{I}_{3} \\\\  & =4C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344706 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345118 1069 "q-75655" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345118 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345118 1072 #uuid "2f25df3d-4b0c-5b67-901d-8d878d2769ac" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345118 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344692 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345119 1069 "q-75659" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345119 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345119 1072 #uuid "0d48ab70-ab46-5142-a432-382f1feb7bb9" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345119 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344858 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345120 1069 "q-75660" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345120 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345120 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345120 1072 #uuid "8d177800-f6a6-59b3-bf6e-5adc772e358a" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345120 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+13{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 13 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(0)+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344615 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345121 1069 "q-75662" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345121 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345121 1071 "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $7\\times 7$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $A(B+C)=AC+AB$\n- $A(BC)=(BA)C$\n- $AB=BA$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345121 1072 #uuid "1c88851c-8567-55d2-897e-8b0f9b020c35" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345121 1074 "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so \n\n$$\nA(B+C)=AB+AC=AC+AB\n$$\n\n since matrix addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC)=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344911 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345122 1069 "q-75664" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345122 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345122 1071 "Given that $A$ and $B$  are $4\\times 4$ matrices,  and ${I}_{4}$ is the $4\\times 4$ identity matrix,  which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $AB=BA$\n- $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A$\n- $A(B+{I}_{4})=AB+A$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345122 1072 #uuid "8619c3de-3523-5f87-a248-dac65df502b6" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345122 1074 "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, however, multiplication by the identity matrix is always commutative. In this case, we have  $A{I}_{4}={I}_{4}A=A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+{I}_{4})=AB+A{I}_{4}=AB+A$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements II  and III are true." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344565 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345123 1069 "q-75717" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345123 1071 "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices which of the following statements are always true? \n\n- $CB=BC$\n- $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- $A(BC)=(AB)C$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345123 1072 #uuid "8ca7b9cd-e052-55a9-b9b9-9f438a40909d" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345123 1074 "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B-C)=AB-AC,$ but $AC\\ne CA$ because matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $A(BC)=(AB)C$ is always true.\n\nTherefore, only statement  III is true." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344643 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345124 1069 "q-75722" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345124 1071 "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{n}^{10}}?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345124 1072 #uuid "6d6a0846-a573-57d3-ae52-bcb10f19c83f" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345124 1074 "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{n}^{10}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{n}^{10}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344831 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345125 1069 "q-75723" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345125 1071 "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}-1$ for $n>1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345125 1072 #uuid "8c99b596-c493-5824-b816-7b21709913d8" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345125 1074 "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}-1)=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{e}^{3n}}=-1+0=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-1.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344597 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345126 1069 "q-75727" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345126 1071 "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{4}}{25{e}^{n}}?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345126 1072 #uuid "0526be40-3312-57da-9d43-a0847eb52729" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345126 1074 "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}}{25{e}^{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{4}}{25{e}^{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $0.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344555 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345127 1069 "q-75729" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345127 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345127 1072 #uuid "7a5c031b-68ff-5d30-80f0-79dec42f9067" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345127 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is false. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=2v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=2{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $2$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{2}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{1}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344700 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345128 1069 "q-75730" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345128 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345128 1072 #uuid "bb537936-d19a-5b11-8425-d86d338ccdb3" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345128 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{4}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{4}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $4$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=4v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $4.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(5v)=5(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=5(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344658 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345129 1069 "q-75733" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345129 1071 "![](images/5e/5eef8e6b1748ba006f06a088ebb03f5ea6fba47941bbef82aa4b7372941bfbe9.png)\n\nThe vector $u$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-3.$ Find the vector $Au.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345129 1072 #uuid "73e16f59-5585-504c-bb8c-79c4ebfada59" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345129 1074 "The eigenvector $u=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au & =-3u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/44/44ce64acad55425e3d0056653ac06cb3fb37d691a425b1ef32e69b29a47074be.png)" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344503 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345130 1069 "q-75734" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345130 1071 "![](images/02/0269c9d60a97984f55cefccbd8cf1192dfdb4082935162206b1ce1a3cfb413f7.png)\n\nThe vector $u$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-1.$ Find the vector $Bu.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345130 1072 #uuid "87b8923c-30bf-57c2-9051-8311487777e0" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345130 1074 "The eigenvector $u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Bu & =-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/af/afc3e8503656a84f23c7f58fd05e005ee07200b75ddec1aa5cf247b3484e81b4.png)" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344836 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345131 1069 "q-7579" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345131 1071 "![](images/b2/b29bf5d439b638bfe8ab3fd013467cab8ef7c393a1cd1cf06461fa5807b6c0ba.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=25$ intersects the line $y=-x+1$ (sketched above)." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345131 1072 #uuid "cfc3b22c-3925-51d7-be47-023293e9f61d" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345131 1074 "To find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we substitute $y=-x+1$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(-x+1-1)^{2} & =25 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(-x)^{2} & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1+{x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-12 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344816 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345132 1069 "q-75800" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345132 1071 "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that \n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2=(x+1)(x-2)P(x).\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345132 1072 #uuid "0610905f-a831-5c19-a024-7010638843b0" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345132 1074 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x+1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-2$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n|  | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2}{x-2}=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2=(x-2)(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1}{x+1}=2{x}^{2}-x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2=(x-2)(x+1)(2{x}^{2}-x+1).\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $2{x}^{2}-x+1$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-1)^{2}-4(2)(1)=-7<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $2{x}^{2}-x+1$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2=(x-2)(x+1){{(2{x}^{2}-x+1)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)=2{x}^{2}-x+1.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344857 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345133 1069 "q-7582" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345133 1071 "![](images/80/80646a56ea6df17b6f0b6460f3d4918174966773b9e7910d252c9098af3b0f48.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x-4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10$ intersects the line $y=x-3$ (sketched above)." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345133 1072 #uuid "d69a0976-bfac-5011-82bc-3bb6b7e045c5" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345133 1074 "To find the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we substitute $y=x-3$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(x-3+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(x-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+16+{x}^{2}-4x+4 & =10 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-12x+10 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344524 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345134 1069 "q-75825" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345134 1071 "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345134 1072 #uuid "33fe88ae-755e-5444-9db1-f4813a03f297" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345134 1074 "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x-8=(x+2)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8} & =\\frac{3x-6}{(x+2)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{x+2}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n3x-6=A(x-4)+B(x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4)-6 & =A(4-4)+B(4+2) \\\\ 6 & =6B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-2)-6 & =A(-2-4)+B(-2+2) \\\\ -12 & =-6A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}=\\frac{2}{x+2}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x+2}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{2}(x-4)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}\\frac{3x-6}{{x}^{2}-2x-8}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+2)^{2}(x-4)|{|}_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344878 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345135 1069 "q-75828" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345135 1071 "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345135 1072 #uuid "36f60655-da6b-51ba-a53b-de029dead882" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345135 1074 "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+16x+3=(5x+1)(x+3).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3} & =\\frac{14}{(5x+1)(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{(5x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+3)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n14=A(x+3)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-3+3)+B(5(-3)+1) \\\\ 14 & =-14B \\\\ B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}+3)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ 14 & =\\frac{14}{5}A \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}=\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{5x+1}-\\frac{1}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{5}{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{11}\\frac{14}{5{x}^{2}+16x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{5x+1}{x+3}|{|}_{4}^{11} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344679 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345136 1069 "q-75831" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345136 1071 "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $-6$ |\n| $3$ | $-3$ | $5$ |" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345136 1072 #uuid "0528b9f3-4cb4-518a-b584-6b0f91a71630" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345136 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=1$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=-6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-6)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344885 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345137 1069 "q-75832" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345137 1071 "The following table gives the values of functions $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({g}^{-1})^{′}(7).$\n\n| **$x$** | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-6$ | $9$ | $-1$ |\n| $4$ | $7$ | $-4$ |" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345137 1072 #uuid "3888632a-e872-565e-a652-d98abc5852ce" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345137 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(7)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(7))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${g}^{-1}(7)=4$ and ${g}^{′}(4)=-4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{-1})^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344761 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345138 1069 "q-75834" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345138 1071 "The following table gives the values of functions $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({g}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n| **$x$** | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $-6$ |\n| $5$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n| $7$ | $-2$ | $2$ |" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345138 1072 #uuid "56955ec1-8e2e-5c6d-ba2e-a589ffad7369" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345138 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({g}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${g}^{-1}(2)=5$ and ${g}^{′}(5)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}({g}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{g}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344529 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345139 1069 "q-75862" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345139 1071 "Find the solution to the  following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345139 1072 #uuid "263b5643-f9a7-58b5-b4f2-be4d8aef81b1" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345139 1074 "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{1}{3}y=2 & |\\times 3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}y=1 & |\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 \\\\ 2x-y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that if we multiply the second equation by $-1,$ then we will get a $y$ term that can cancel with the $-y$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 &  \\\\ 2x-y=4 & |\\,\\times (-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=6 \\\\ -2x+y=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-\\cancel{y} & =6 \\\\ -2x+\\cancel{y} & =-4 \\\\ (6x-2x) & =(6-4) \\\\ 4x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equation to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =4 \\\\ 2(\\frac{1}{2})-y & =4 \\\\ 1-y & =4 \\\\ y & =1-4 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-3.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344879 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345140 1069 "q-75924" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345140 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x}=4.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345140 1072 #uuid "19f3a0d4-a9e3-5752-9f3d-c41b8d14446d" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345140 1074 "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x} & =4 \\\\ x\\cdot (\\frac{x+7}{x}-\\frac{1-x}{x}) & =x\\cdot 4 \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{x+7}{x}-x\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{x} & =x\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x+7}{\\cancel{x}}-\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{\\cancel{x}} & =4x \\\\ x+7-(1-x) & =4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7-(1-x) & =4x \\\\ 2x+6 & =4x \\\\ 4x-2x & =6 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=3\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=3.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344604 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345141 1069 "q-75925" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345141 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x-2}{5x}=\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345141 1072 #uuid "dd8936d7-9acb-5f42-aac4-919b57567005" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345141 1074 "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $5x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{5x} & =\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5 \\\\ 5x\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{5x}) & =5x\\cdot (\\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5) \\\\ 5x\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{5x} & =5x\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{5x}+5x\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{5x}} & =\\cancel{5x}\\cdot \\frac{3-20x}{\\cancel{5x}}+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3-20x+25x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =3-20x+25x \\\\ x-2 & =3+5x \\\\ 5x-x & =-2-3 \\\\ 4x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-\\frac{5}{4}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{5}{4}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344862 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345142 1069 "q-75930" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345142 1071 "Solve for $a$ where $\\frac{a+3}{3a}=\\frac{a-1}{a}+2.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345142 1072 #uuid "18e01818-579e-59cf-ab50-d0abd274fe70" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345142 1074 "We note that $a=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $a$ and $3a.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(a,3a)=3a.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3a,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a+3}{3a} & =\\frac{a-1}{a}+2 \\\\ 3a\\cdot (\\frac{a+3}{3a}) & =3a\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{a}+2) \\\\ 3a\\cdot \\frac{a+3}{3a} & =3a\\cdot \\frac{a-1}{a}+3a\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{3a}\\cdot \\frac{a+3}{\\cancel{3a}} & =3\\cancel{a}\\cdot \\frac{a-1}{\\cancel{a}}+6a \\\\ a+3 & =3(a-1)+6a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+3 & =3(a-1)+6a \\\\ a+3 & =9a-3 \\\\ 9a-a & =3+3 \\\\ 8a & =6 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. But since $a=\\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344627 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345143 1069 "q-75941" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345143 1071 "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m}=\\frac{m+4}{15m}?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345143 1072 #uuid "64167e5a-a3e2-55d1-956c-8945cc13aa71" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345143 1074 "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3m,5m,$ and $15m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3m,5m,15m)=15m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $15m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m} & =\\frac{m+4}{15m} \\\\ 15m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m}+\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 15m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m})+15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3m\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{3m})+3\\cdot 5m\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{5m}) & =15m\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{15m}) \\\\ 5\\cdot \\cancel{3m}\\cdot (\\frac{2m-3}{\\cancel{3m}})+3\\cdot \\cancel{5m}\\cdot (\\frac{m+5}{\\cancel{5m}}) & =\\cancel{15m}\\cdot (\\frac{m+4}{\\cancel{15m}}) \\\\ 5(2m-3)+3(m+5) & =m+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2m-3)+3(m+5) & =m+4 \\\\ 10m-15+3m+15 & =m+4 \\\\ 13m & =m+4 \\\\ 12m & =4 \\\\ m & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=\\frac{1}{3}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344628 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345144 1069 "q-75944" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345144 1071 "The matrix $D$ is given below. Let ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, and ${d}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $D.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(D).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${d}_{3}={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{2}{d}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -7 & -17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345144 1072 #uuid "29f3d15c-3699-5650-acaa-cf842bca8086" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345144 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $D$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -7 & -17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{8}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is ${{d}_{1},{d}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{3}}={{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{1}}+{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 2=6.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344584 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345145 1069 "q-75946" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345145 1071 "The matrix $P$ is given below. Let ${p}_{1}$, ${p}_{2}$, and ${p}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $P.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${p}_{3}={x}_{1}{p}_{1}+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345145 1072 #uuid "edfd11b7-178d-5b9f-89aa-f777ae4db963" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345145 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $P$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$ is ${{p}_{1},{p}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${p}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{3}}={{(-2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{1}}+{{(1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-2)\\cdot 1=-2.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344849 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345146 1069 "q-75948" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345146 1071 "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10$ for $n\\ge 1?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345146 1072 #uuid "2bb3e1d8-7bd7-5a1e-9e4a-ce861b38aceb" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345146 1074 "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10)=10+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n)}+10)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344650 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345147 1069 "q-7595" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345147 1071 "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345147 1072 #uuid "e4e0a287-1212-5e98-875c-b8a10ba64112" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345147 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=9.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be  $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=9,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3=6.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344909 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345148 1069 "q-75952" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345148 1071 "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{20\\operatorname{log}⁡n}{{n}^{2}-1}$ for $n>1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345148 1072 #uuid "e0b69239-091d-5d0d-8b29-c14dd80b538a" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345148 1074 "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{20\\operatorname{log}⁡n}{{n}^{2}-1}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{20\\operatorname{log}⁡n}{{n}^{2}-1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344770 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345149 1069 "q-75956" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345149 1071 "The matrix $W$ is given below. Let ${w}_{1}$, ${w}_{2}$, and ${w}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $W.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(W).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${w}_{2}={x}_{1}{w}_{1}+{x}_{3}{w}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345149 1072 #uuid "8e38527f-7273-52c9-bec3-d94fc3af2837" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345149 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is ${{w}_{1},{w}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{2}}={{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344587 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345150 1069 "q-75962" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345150 1071 "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345150 1072 #uuid "c7877136-a6fe-51f9-a9fa-720076528d75" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345150 1074 "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}{{e}^{3n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344750 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345151 1069 "q-75963" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345151 1071 "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}{3{e}^{n}}+1$ for $n\\ge 1?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345151 1072 #uuid "233ff312-428a-52f7-93ce-21d31b250464" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345151 1074 "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}{3{e}^{n}}+1)=1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}{3{e}^{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}{3{e}^{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}{3{e}^{n}}=1+0=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $1.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344744 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345152 1069 "q-75972" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345152 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345152 1072 #uuid "1bf0179e-ce2c-5f32-9679-e1717a127638" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345152 1074 "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,-6\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,4\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=(-6)\\cdot {b}_{1}+4\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{1}=-6.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344707 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345153 1069 "q-75973" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345153 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345153 1072 #uuid "cfe9fa20-a503-5468-a775-31a4b970b2ec" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345153 1074 "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,4\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,-3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=4\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-3)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ \\,\\,-3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-3.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344904 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345154 1069 "q-75976" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345154 1071 "![](images/f7/f74ae80071413963d1b71f91b99ae625edf6e285bb068be7b969483672f1288e.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x+2)(x-4)\\ge 0.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345154 1072 #uuid "6f1a6ebf-461a-5edb-8189-a41795a3f536" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345154 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x+2)(x-4)\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-(x+2)(x-4)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2b/2bcb7336f352b1730a9c5b52060a4d49e9209cac73ca2688717f68c1af73168b.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-2\\le x\\le 4.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344774 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345155 1069 "q-75977" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345155 1071 "The matrix $H$ is given below. Let ${h}_{1}$, ${h}_{2}$,  ${h}_{3}$ and ${h}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $H.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${h}_{4}={x}_{1}{h}_{1}+{x}_{2}{h}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 12 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345155 1072 #uuid "e2ee7dc8-6fbf-5fd7-b2a6-20aab9680dc7" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345155 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $H$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 12 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$ is ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2},{h}_{3}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{h}_{1},{h}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344943 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345156 1069 "q-75978" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345156 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345156 1072 #uuid "32d360b2-b07c-58ea-8316-13ed526cce1e" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345156 1074 "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,-8\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,4\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}+{{(\\,2\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=-8\\cdot {b}_{1}+4\\cdot {b}_{2}+2\\cdot {b}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{2}=4.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344800 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345157 1069 "q-75980" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345157 1071 "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${v}_{2}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345157 1072 #uuid "c466a2ff-d594-54f8-8655-5aeb347fd10d" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345157 1074 "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{10}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{4}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344768 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345158 1069 "q-75984" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345158 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{3}?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345158 1072 #uuid "4061dc6a-87c8-53d1-aa6a-18325afc93ad" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345158 1074 "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={{(\\,-2\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{{(\\,-1\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}+{{(\\,5\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{3}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=-2\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-1)\\cdot {b}_{2}+5\\cdot {b}_{3}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{3}=5.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344760 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345159 1069 "q-75987" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345159 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345159 1072 #uuid "7a0673ad-ec47-547a-a169-9262732d993a" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345159 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x+1 & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344573 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345160 1069 "q-75989" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345160 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345160 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345160 1072 #uuid "4d18b23f-7f2c-58d6-9c7b-1f111a7cab73" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345160 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 0=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the third equation is always false. Therefore, the system has no solution and we conclude that $x\\notin \\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344753 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345161 1069 "q-75991" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345161 1071 "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}-3x+4<0.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345161 1072 #uuid "cd33e6bb-5aa7-5e5e-9f8b-a5ae4029a3d3" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345161 1074 "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}-3x+4<0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}-3x+4\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-3x+4 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}+3x-4) & =0 \\\\ -(x+4)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-4$ and $x=1,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/6a/6a5528147c994e180c2ffb41bd259dae489a3fc02fa1d6c77452d6cf1722e9be.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-4$ or $x>1.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344823 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345162 1069 "q-75992" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345162 1071 "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}-2x+15>0.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345162 1072 #uuid "ca52724e-95d0-555c-b582-e5e500954e40" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345162 1074 "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}-2x+15>0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}-2x+15\n$$\n\nlies *above* the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-2x+15 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}+2x-15) & =0 \\\\ -(x+5)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-5$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/da/da88a1971a7a389c8f5b59ebd3cef2a146eb09113c3e71031bf9dfb6cdbe91e1.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *above* the axis. \n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-5<x<3.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344649 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345163 1069 "q-7601" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345163 1071 "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345163 1072 #uuid "548834cd-5f39-5629-b131-aad9f4ac51b6" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345163 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344632 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345164 1069 "q-7602" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345164 1071 "![](images/bc/bcac8eb60e0603ee38bd200b15072685a2bcebb834d490fb4e5a0c318f401096.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345164 1072 #uuid "802974bf-a2fe-59d8-9a6a-a2db7dc5159c" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345164 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=1$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344509 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345165 1069 "q-76043" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345165 1071 "If $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1-10\\text{i} \\\\ -7-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ then $\\,\\overset{―}{x}=$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345165 1072 #uuid "a6cd6f3a-356f-564a-8730-711a3f659a57" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345165 1074 "To find the complex conjugate of a vector, we take the complex conjugate of the entries of the vector. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\overset{―}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1-10\\text{i} \\\\ -7-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{6} \\\\ \\overset{―}{1-10\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{-7-3\\text{i}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1+10\\text{i} \\\\ -7+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344875 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345166 1069 "q-76044" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345166 1071 "If $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 2-2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ then $\\,\\overset{―}{x}=$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345166 1072 #uuid "fddc67c9-7cd5-52c0-962f-1c7e4a044655" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345166 1074 "To find the complex conjugate of a vector, we take the complex conjugate of the entries of the vector. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\overset{―}{[\\begin{aligned}4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 2-2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{4+3\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{2-2\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{5\\text{i}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2+2\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344739 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345167 1069 "q-76048" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345167 1071 "If $\\,\\text{Re}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\text{Im}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are the real and imaginary parts of the vector $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}8+\\text{i} \\\\ -5+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ then what is the value of $ac-bd?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345167 1072 #uuid "6a3b677a-08b2-5263-b320-cb9f44b6bd97" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345167 1074 "For the given vector $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3-5\\text{i} \\\\ 6-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =[\\begin{aligned}8+\\text{i} \\\\ -5+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(x)}+\\text{i}\\,{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\,a=8,b=-5,c=1$ and $d=3$, which implies that \n\n$$\nac-bd=8\\cdot 1-(-5)\\cdot 3=23.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344914 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345168 1069 "q-76049" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345168 1071 "If $\\,\\text{Re}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\text{Im}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are the real and imaginary parts of the vector $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-4+9\\text{i} \\\\ 1+8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ then what is the value of $bc-ad?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345168 1072 #uuid "52941a01-ea01-5908-9173-c9bb5ba6d39c" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345168 1074 "For the given vector $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-4+9\\text{i} \\\\ 1+8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+9\\text{i} \\\\ 1+8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(x)}+\\text{i}\\,{{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\,a=-4,b=1,c=9$ and $d=8$, which implies that \n\n$$\nbc-ad=1\\cdot 9-(-4)\\cdot 8=41.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344699 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345169 1069 "q-76050" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345169 1071 "If $\\,\\text{Re}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\text{Im}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are the real and imaginary parts of the vector $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-6+7\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ then what is the value of $ad+bc?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345169 1072 #uuid "092a46e1-ab30-51e7-8a06-0f91ed1c7242" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345169 1074 "For the given vector $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-6+7\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =[\\begin{aligned}-6+7\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(x)}+\\text{i}\\,{{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\,a=-6,b=1,c=7$ and $d=-6$, which implies that \n\n$$\nad+bc=(-6)\\cdot (-6)+1\\cdot 7=43.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344612 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345170 1069 "q-76052" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345170 1071 "Find the imaginary part of the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}4\\text{i}+5 \\\\ -8\\text{i}+7\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345170 1072 #uuid "ad4a8de6-1917-5de3-bf8b-9b85c988593f" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345170 1074 "For the given vector $\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}4\\text{i}+5 \\\\ -8\\text{i}+7\\end{aligned}]\\,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\text{i}+5 \\\\ -8\\text{i}+7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} \\\\ 7-8\\tex