{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 35{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344677 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345241 1069 "q-76601" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345241 1071 "If $\\,u(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,v(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$, find $Aw(t)$ where $w(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{7t} \\\\ 3{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345241 1072 #uuid "61125cb1-443b-53be-8d39-14bb208d8381" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345241 1074 "Since $u(t)$ and $v(t)$ are solutions to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ the superposition principle guarantees that \n\n$$\ncu(t)+dv(t)\n$$\n\n is also a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for all $c,d\\in R.$\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\nw(t)=2u(t)+3v(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $w$ is a solution to ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ So, in order to find $Aw(t)$ we just need to find ${w}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Aw & ={w}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{7t})^{′} \\\\ (3{e}^{-2t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14{e}^{7t} \\\\ -6{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344759 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345242 1069 "q-76605" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345242 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8})\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(2,4).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345242 1072 #uuid "2db69cc9-2b8f-5c01-a877-0e37bcf27dc2" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345242 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(2,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+j$ and $b=2\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+j)+t[2\\,i+4\\,j-(i+j)] \\\\  & =(i+j)+t(i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\,i+(1+3t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+t$ and $y=1+3t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)j \\\\  & =1\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi ((1+3t)-(1+t))}{8}) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4})\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\frac{\\pi t}{4},$ $\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344576 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345243 1069 "q-76610" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345243 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345243 1072 #uuid "d123bbf4-7b8c-5b08-b206-9ffde691a39b" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345243 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-2{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344737 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345244 1069 "q-76616" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345244 1071 "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ along the polar curve $r=2\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345244 1072 #uuid "5897420e-efe0-5c2e-8d46-6165bb9e5dd3" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345244 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$  \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi /2)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344504 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345245 1069 "q-76641" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345245 1070 #edb/re