\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344684 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345247 1069 "q-76650" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345247 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(4x+2)\\,{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3).$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345247 1072 #uuid "e2968de0-3f24-5e15-95ba-b17804d225c1" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345247 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t[2\\,i+3\\,j-j] \\\\  & =j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=(4x+2)\\,{e}^{xy}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4(2t)+2){e}^{2t(1+2t)} \\\\  & =(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t+4{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=(2+8t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344880 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345248 1069 "q-76658" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345248 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(2y+3x)\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345248 1072 #uuid "e85bae9c-7a72-56f0-9f05-0164e98daa66" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345248 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2y+3x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(0+3)-(-2+0) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344536 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345249 1069 "q-76660" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345249 1071 "$6\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7})=$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345249 1072 #uuid "3d7f3236-aee5-5d63-80b7-2ecd442664e7" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345249 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{7})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7}) & =3\\cdot [2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{7})]\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{7})} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344566 13194