\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 4\\end{aligned}|-8\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344722 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345349 1069 "q-7782" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345349 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=0.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345349 1072 #uuid "90a25429-7c22-5d0a-a1ee-3b92bacbd5ae" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345349 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344596 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345350 1069 "q-7783" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345350 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345350 1072 #uuid "4721b7fe-866d-52da-8089-b7acfe28a0b8" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345350 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-3{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344593 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345351 1069 "q-77864" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345351 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]?$\n\n- $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$\n- $g(x)=|{x}^{2}+1|$\n- $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345351 1072 #uuid "b0acb71b-a2ed-51d8-8bca-93474ee1c192" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345351 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|{x}^{2}+1|$ is equivalent to $p(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ so it is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, the functions I, II, and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344938 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345352 1069 "q-77875" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345352 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,5]?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345352 1072 #uuid "e5a8930f-9a90-55dc-adf0-15dc765d8f1b" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345352 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,5]$ and differentiable on $(-1,5).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,5]$ and differentiable on $(-1,5)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,5].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$  is continuous on $[-1,5]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=2.$  Hence, $g(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,5).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, $q(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,5].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,5]$ but has corner points at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ Hence, $r(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,5).$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344600 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345353 1069 "q-77888" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345353 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,3]?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345353 1072 #uuid "5cb19f75-0bba-5852-9c01-556fa7367f72" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345353 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $h(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=2.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $r(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, $t(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem." 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344777 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345354 1069 "q-77893" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345354 1071 "Consider the line $2x-y-4=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345354 1072 #uuid "836e83a5-a45a-5ec2-8875-53cb69e61091" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345354 1074 "First, notice that the line $2x-y-4=0$ passes through the points $R(0,-4)$ and $S(1,-2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $R$ and $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${R}^{′}(8,-12)$ and ${S}^{′}(3,-6).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${R}^{′}$ and ${S}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-12) & =\\frac{-6-(-12)}{3-8}(x-8) \\\\ y+12 & =-\\frac{6}{5}(x-8) \\\\ 5y+60 & =-6x+48 \\\\ 6x+5y+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344833 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345355 1069 "q-7790" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345355 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}-14y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(1)=5.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345355 1072 #uuid "fdf2426a-3d50-5569-9ef4-40597922e38a" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345355 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-14{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -14) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -7)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =7.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA{e}^{-2}+B{e}^{7}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+7B{e}^{7x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-2A{e}^{-2}+7B{e}^{7}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-2}+B{e}^{7}=2 \\\\ -2A{e}^{-2}+7B{e}^{7}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A={e}^{2},B={e}^{-7}.$\n\nNow we get the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-2x}+{e}^{-7}\\cdot {e}^{7x} \\\\ y & ={e}^{-2x+2}+{e}^{7x-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344720 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345356 1069 "q-7793" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345356 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=0.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345356 1072 #uuid "57c8d59c-5a1b-5109-ad52-f9764b6aac17" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345356 1074 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+16{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +4) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-4$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344579 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345357 1069 "q-77949" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345357 1071 "Given that ${i,j,k}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ and that the images of $i,$ $j,$ and $k$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨3,4,-1⟩,$ $⟨2,0,7⟩,$ and $⟨-4,-2,9⟩,$ respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345357 1072 #uuid "7cb18e0b-ad76-527b-af2e-d6245b5f36e8" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345357 1074 "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{3}$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i,j,$ and $k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 7 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344928 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345358 1069 "q-77950" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345358 1071 "Given that ${i,j,k}$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ and that the images of $i,$ $j,$ and $k$ under the action of a linear transformation $T$ are $⟨6,2,0⟩,$ $⟨-3,5,6⟩,$ and $⟨-1,2,7⟩,$ respectively, what is the standard matrix of $T?$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345358 1072 #uuid "cf91244f-b592-5bd1-a790-f40103636b32" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true] [17592186345358 1074 "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{3}$ can be represented as a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i,j,$ and $k$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-000413bf-9748-547e-b2b7-f51e9ca2b6c3" 17592186342250 "answer-005d0dc0-c679-52c6-abbc-b50e1a9c606d" 17592186342251 "answer-005e6ee0-d7cb-5738-ae39-b5d2d1580ad4" 175921863